.TH STICKYFS 4
.SH NAME
stickyfs \- sticky's notes as files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stickyfs
[
.B -h
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Stickyfs
keeps the notes of
.IR sticky (1):
their text and its undo, their colors and their rectangles.
and their windows.
Each note has one window, a
.IR rio (1)
window that
.I stickyfs
starts as a process of its own, and each window is a view of the server's note;
.IR sticky (1)
describes the windows.
At start
.I stickyfs
reads the notes in
.BR $home/lib/sticky ,
posts
.BI /srv/sticky. user\fR,
sets
.B $stickysrv
to it,
mounts its control files on
.B /mnt/sticky
and its notes on
.B $home/lib/sticky
in the name space it was started in,
and opens a window for each note, where the note was.
With
.B -h
it opens none until a write of
.B show
to
.B /mnt/sticky/ctl
opens them.
A
.I stickyfs
that finds another running, by its
.B /srv
file, mounts that one in its own place and exits;
of two started at once, the one that posts first serves, and the other mounts it.
A
.B /srv
file whose server is gone is removed.
.I Stickyfs
runs in a note group of its own, so that the shell that started it can go.
.PP
.B $home/lib/sticky
holds, for each note
.IR n ,
.TF n.meta
.TP
.IB n .md
the note's Markdown.
A read gives the text as it was at the open.
What is written replaces the note's text when the file closes, as one change that
.B undo
takes back, so that
.BR cp ,
.B >
and
.B >>
work as on a disk file.
A remove takes the note away, its window and its files on the disk, as
.B Del
in its window does.
A create of
.IB n .md\fR,
for a number
.I n
no note has, makes a note and opens its window;
for a note's number, it opens the note's file for writing, as a write with
.B >
does.
.TP
.IB n .meta
lines
.BI color " name"
and
.BI rect " x0 y0 x1 y1\fR,
the note's color and its window's rectangle;
lines written to it set them when the file closes.
.PD
.PP
Under the mount the server keeps each note in the directory beneath, at the same names,
which it reaches through a name space of its own made before the mount:
it writes
.IB n .md
a moment after a change and
.IB n .meta
when the color or rectangle change,
and these are what the next
.I stickyfs
reads.
It writes each to
.IB n .md.new
first and renames it, so that a failed write leaves the note as it was;
at start it finishes a rename that a crash cut short.
It takes the numbers it loaded and the free ones, never a number that has a file beneath.
An edit made to a note's file beneath the mount, by a window that lacks the mount,
lasts until the server next writes that note, which replaces it;
a new file put there keeps its number and stays, untouched,
until the next
.I stickyfs
loads it.
A name such as
.B 01.md
is not a note's, and a note's file holding a NUL is left alone.
.PP
.B /mnt/sticky
is after
.IR acme (4):
.TF new/ctl
.TP
.B ctl
takes
.BR exit ,
which writes every note with text, removes the empty ones, ends every window
and exits, writing the notes again just before it goes; and
.BR show ,
which opens a window for each note that has none.
.TP
.B index
a line for each note: its number, its length in runes, whether it is dirty, its color and its name,
.IB n .md
or, for a window on a file, the file's name.
.TP
.B new/ctl
an open makes a note,
.IR n ,
and is then
.IB n /ctl\fR;
the note is empty, has no window until
.B show
asks for one, and goes when the file closes if it is still empty and windowless.
.TP
.IB n /body
the text; a read gives it all and a write appends.
.TP
.IB n /addr
an address, written as
.BI # q0 , # q1\fR,
.BI # q\fR,
.B $
or
.BR , ,
and read as two numbers, for
.BR data .
.TP
.IB n /data
a write replaces the addressed text and leaves the address after it;
a read gives the addressed text.
.TP
.IB n /seq
the note's sequence number, one more for each change.
A number written to it guards the next open of
.B data
or
.B body
for writing, which fails if the note has changed since;
the guard stays with the open file, and a write to it fails if anyone else has changed the
note since its open or its last write, as in
.IR acme (4).
.TP
.IB n /ctl
a read gives the number, the length, dirty, the color, the rectangle,
.B note
or
.B file
and a file's name.
Writes, one a line, take
.BI color " name"
(the six names of
.IR sticky (1)
or
.BI # rrggbb\fR),
.BI rect " x0 y0 x1 y1\fR,
.B undo
and
.BR redo ,
.B nomark
(the next write to
.B data
is part of the last change, for undo),
.B mark
(cancel that),
.B put
(write the note to the disk now),
.B delete
(remove the note and end its window) and
.B show
(open a window for the note if it has none).
.BI file " name"
makes a note fresh from
.B new/ctl
a window on a file, as
.B sticky
.I file
does, so that
.B exit
ends it too; it fails on a note with text, a file or a window.
Such a note has only
.B ctl
and
.BR rdevent ,
and goes when its window does.
.TP
.IB n /rdevent
the note's changes, for any number of readers, one to a read, in acme's form:
.BI FD q0 " " q1 " 0 0"
for a deletion,
.BI FI q0 " " q1 " 0 " nr " " text
for an insertion
.RI ( nr
0 for a long one),
and
.B Sc0 0 0 0
when the color or dirty changes, for a reread of
.BR ctl .
A reader is the note's window: while one is open the note is shown.
When the last closes, a note with no text, or a window on a file, goes;
a note with text whose window lived a few seconds, as one rio deletes,
gets a new window, and one whose window ended at once waits for
.BR show .
A read gives end of file once the note is gone or the server exits.
.PD
.SH FILES
.TF /srv/sticky.user
.TP
.BI /srv/sticky. user
the server
.TP
.B $home/lib/sticky/*.md
the notes, beneath the mount, which survive a reboot
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/sticky
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sticky (1),
.IR acme (4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
If a note can't be written, a write of
.B exit
fails with the reason and
.I stickyfs
keeps serving the notes; if a write fails as it goes, it prints the reason and keeps serving
them, with their windows ended, until
.B show
or another
.BR exit .
.SH BUGS
Files in
.B $home/lib/sticky
other than the notes' are hidden while
.I stickyfs
runs.
.PP
Windows that rio makes from its menu start in rio's name space, without the mount;
.B stickyfs
run in one mounts the running server there.
